# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
kant-groundwork-2.md:1: argue/readings: S8 is read 2 ways: kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1 has "universal_law → nobody_believes" and "nobody_believes → ¬promise_possible ∧ ¬end_attainable"; kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3 have "□(universal_law → nobody_believes)" and "□(nobody_believes → ¬promise_possible ∧ ¬end_attainable)"; they differ when only "the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature", "no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences" and "necessarily, (if the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature, then no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences)" are true, which the first allows
kant-groundwork-2.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S8 is read kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1 with no modality; kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "should" or "might" does not say which it means
kant-groundwork-2.md:1: argue/answered: S8: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature", "no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences", "necessarily, (if the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature, then no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences)" and "necessarily, (if the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature, then no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences)" are true and "the promise itself is possible", "the end that one might have in view in the promise is attainable", "necessarily, (if no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences, then (it is not so that the promise itself is possible and it is not so that the end that one might have in view in the promise is attainable))" and "necessarily, (if no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences, then (it is not so that the promise itself is possible and it is not so that the end that one might have in view in the promise is attainable))" are false. Is S8, "For supposing it to be a universal law that everyone when he thinks himself in a difficulty should be able to promise whatever he pleases, with the purpose of not keeping his promise, the promise itself would become impossible, as well as the end that one might have in view in it, since no one would consider that anything was promised to him, but would ridicule all such statements as vain pretences.", true then?
kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1 has "universal_law → promise_possible"; kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3 have "□(universal_law → promise_possible)"; they differ when only "the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature" and "the promise itself is possible" are true, which the first allows
kant-groundwork-2.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1 has "¬universal_law"; kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3 have "¬◇universal_law"; they differ when only "possibly, the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature" is true, which the first allows
kant-groundwork-2.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1 with no modality; kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "could" does not say which it means
kant-groundwork-2.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "possibly, the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature" and "possibly, the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature" are true and "the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature" is false. Is the conclusion, "Then I see at once that it could never hold as a universal law of nature, but would necessarily contradict itself.", true then?
kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3 take for granted "necessarily, (if the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature, then the promise itself is possible)"; kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1 does not
kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.2 and kant-groundwork-2.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "necessarily, (if the maxim of promising falsely when in difficulty holds as a universal law of nature, then the promise itself is possible)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
